# Quorum backend — Cloud Run service definition template.
#
# REWRITTEN (`DEC-159`) to describe the real, live deployment, replacing a
# stale, aspirational version written before a real Cloud Run project
# existed. That original version was never updated once real deployment
# actually happened (`DEC-098` onward) -- it still named a placeholder
# `PROJECT_ID`, the wrong region (`us-central1`, not the real, live
# `asia-south1` this project's own co-location rule requires), the wrong
# `--max-instances` (10, not the real, live 2), and `--no-allow-
# unauthenticated` (superseded by `DEC-102` once the real login system
# became the actual security boundary). This version is a real, faithful
# description of the live service as of `quorum-backend-00026-chc`, not
# a forward-looking plan for a deployment that doesn't exist yet.
#
# Real project: `quorum-505909`. Real service: `quorum-backend`, region
# `asia-south1`. Confirmed directly via `gcloud run services describe`
# before writing this file, not reconstructed from memory or an old doc.
#
# `--allow-unauthenticated` is correct and current (`DEC-102`) -- the
# real, live login system (`DEC-101`, Bearer-token auth on every endpoint
# that needs one) is the real security boundary now, not Cloud Run's own
# network layer.
#
# SECRET HANDLING (`DEC-159`): six of the real credentials this service
# needs are Secret Manager references (`secretKeyRef`, real IAM-scoped
# access for the service's own runtime service account,
# `649581407643-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com`) -- the six
# ranked highest by real blast-radius if leaked, chosen under a real,
# hard constraint: Secret Manager's own free tier allows exactly 6 active
# secret versions per month project-wide (confirmed directly, not
# assumed, before this migration -- see `DEC-159`'s own log entry for the
# full ranking and reasoning). The remaining real credentials below stay
# as plain environment variables, deliberately -- not overlooked:
# `SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY` (confirmed unused anywhere in this backend's own
# code, and its real value is a Supabase "publishable"-type key, non-
# secret by that provider's own design), `GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` (not
# confidential per the OAuth spec itself), `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` (public
# by that provider's own design), `UPSTASH_REDIS_URL` (a plain endpoint
# hostname, not a credential), plus `GROQ_API_KEY`/`TAVILY_API_KEY`/
# `UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN`/`LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` -- real, billed/
# access credentials that would ideally also move to Secret Manager, left
# as plain env vars only because of the real 6-secret budget, a
# real, disclosed, deliberately incomplete migration rather than a
# silently-declared-finished one. A future session could migrate more of
# these once/if the free-tier budget allows, or accept the small real
# cost (~$0.06/secret/month) of exceeding it -- a genuine, disclosed open
# item, not silently deferred without a record.

apiVersion: serving.knative.dev/v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: quorum-backend
  # region: asia-south1 -- matches Supabase's own real region
  # (ap-south-1/Mumbai) per this project's explicit co-location rule
  # (CLAUDE.md architecture fact) -- confirmed live, not assumed.
spec:
  template:
    metadata:
      annotations:
        # concurrency=1, explicit -- closes a real cross-user
        # state-isolation risk (CLAUDE.md architecture fact). Never raised
        # without a documented reason.
        autoscaling.knative.dev/maxScale: "2"
        # minScale is deliberately absent here -- CRITICAL-tier review
        # (`DEC-159`) confirmed the live service carries no explicit
        # `minScale` annotation at all; `0` (genuine scale-to-zero) is
        # Cloud Run's own real default when the annotation is omitted,
        # not something this service sets explicitly. Stated as prose
        # above, not as a YAML key this template doesn't actually carry
        # live.
        run.googleapis.com/startup-cpu-boost: "true"
    spec:
      containerConcurrency: 1
      containers:
        # A real, specific tag, not `:latest` -- CRITICAL-tier review
        # (`DEC-159`) confirmed this Artifact Registry repository has no
        # `:latest` tag at all; every real deploy in this project's
        # history has used a real, dated tag (`decNNN-YYYYMMDD`, see
        # DECISIONS_LOG.md's own deploy-record entries). Shown here as
        # the real tag live at the time this template was last verified
        # against the actual service -- expect this to be stale the
        # moment the next real deploy happens; it's illustrative, not
        # load-bearing (a real deploy always names its own real tag
        # explicitly, per the CLI commands below, never relies on this
        # file being current).
        - image: asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/quorum-505909/cloud-run-source-deploy/quorum-backend:dec153-20260829
          resources:
            limits:
              cpu: "1"
              memory: 512Mi
          env:
            # Six real Secret Manager references (`DEC-159`) -- the
            # highest-blast-radius credentials this service holds.
            - name: JWT_SIGNING_KEY
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: jwt-signing-key
                  key: latest
            - name: SUPABASE_URL
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: supabase-url
                  key: latest
            - name: GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: google-token-encryption-key
                  key: latest
            - name: GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: google-oauth-client-secret
                  key: latest
            - name: INTERNAL_DRAIN_SECRET
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: internal-drain-secret
                  key: latest
            - name: GEMINI_API_KEY
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: gemini-api-key
                  key: latest
            # Real, plain environment variables -- see the file header
            # for exactly why each of these deliberately isn't a Secret
            # Manager reference.
            - name: GROQ_API_KEY
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: TAVILY_API_KEY
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: UPSTASH_REDIS_URL
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"
            - name: LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY
              value: "REDACTED -- real value set via --set-env-vars at deploy time, never committed here"

# Equivalent real gcloud CLI commands -- the actual, current shape of
# this deployment, not the declarative YAML above (this service has
# always been deployed via `gcloud run deploy`/`gcloud run services
# update`, never `gcloud run services replace` against this file
# directly -- this template documents the real shape, it isn't itself
# applied).
#
# A fresh build + deploy (source code changed):
#   gcloud builds submit --tag asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/quorum-505909/cloud-run-source-deploy/quorum-backend:<tag>
#   gcloud run deploy quorum-backend \
#     --image asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/quorum-505909/cloud-run-source-deploy/quorum-backend:<tag> \
#     --region asia-south1 \
#     --concurrency 1 \
#     --min-instances 0 \
#     --max-instances 2 \
#     --allow-unauthenticated
#   (real env vars/secrets persist across a plain `gcloud run deploy`
#   with no --set-env-vars/--update-secrets flags, since they're already
#   attached to the service -- only pass those flags when a credential
#   value itself is actually changing.)
#
# A config-only change (no image rebuild), e.g. rotating a Secret
# Manager-backed credential's value:
#
#   OLD_VERSION=$(gcloud secrets versions list jwt-signing-key --filter="state:ENABLED" --format="value(name)")
#   printf '%s' "$NEW_VALUE" | gcloud secrets versions add jwt-signing-key --data-file=-
#   gcloud secrets versions destroy "$OLD_VERSION" --secret=jwt-signing-key
#
#   THE `versions destroy` STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL (CRITICAL-tier review
#   finding, `DEC-159`): this project sits at exactly 6/6 of Secret
#   Manager's real free-tier active-version budget, with zero headroom --
#   the same real constraint `DEC-159` names as the reason only 6 of this
#   service's 14 real credentials were migrated at all. Google's own
#   pricing terms bill a secret version as "active" in BOTH its Enabled
#   AND Disabled states -- only `versions destroy` actually frees the
#   slot. Running `versions add` alone, even followed by `versions
#   disable`, silently pushes this project to 7 active versions and a
#   real, small ($0.06/month) cost this project's own "free-tier only"
#   constraint explicitly forbids. `destroy` is irreversible for that one
#   version (the OLD value becomes permanently unrecoverable) -- correct
#   here specifically because rotation means the old value is deliberately
#   being retired, never wanted back.
#
#   No redeploy needed at all if the service references the secret as
#   `:latest` -- the next real cold start picks up the new version
#   automatically. A deliberate, real redeploy, e.g. to force immediate
#   rollover on every current warm instance, uses
#   `gcloud run services update quorum-backend --region asia-south1`
#   with no other flags -- creates a new revision from the same image.
